Imposter Phenomenon (Marginalization-Driven) - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Occupational problem)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Fraudulence belief, Stereotype threat fear, Over-performance exhaustion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-022
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of severe atypical chest pain, presyncope, and profound physical exhaustion secondary to extreme occupational over-performance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of chest tightness, extreme dizziness upon standing, and a heart rate over 115 bpm following a 72-hour period of continuous work. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out a stress-induced cardiomyopathy or acute ischemic event prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest feels incredibly tight and I keep blacking out when I stand, but I cannot stop working or they'll realize I don't belong here.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic, persistent, internalized belief of intellectual fraudulence despite objective success, heavily driven by their marginalized identity in a corporate environment. Over the past 72 hours, an intense fear of confirming negative stereotypes (stereotype threat fear) triggered extreme hyper-preparation for a routine presentation, resulting in profound sleep deprivation and continuous work. The patient notes a chronic attribution of personal achievements to diversity quotas, reluctance to ask for help out of fear of exposing perceived deficits, minimization of praise, and high baseline anxiety regarding impending exposure. This unsustainable drive has culminated tonight in acute physical burnout, characterized by severe over-performance exhaustion, palpitations, and atypical chest pressure.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; unable to safely stand or complete basic self-care due to severe orthostatic dizziness and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dangerous physiological collapse and occupational burnout due to complete lack of boundaries and self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, visibly trembling, heavily caffeinated, and surrounded by work materials.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia. Significant orthostatic drop noted (BP drops to 90/55 on standing, accompanied by severe dizziness). Normal heart sounds, no murmurs. Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally, slightly rapid but unlabored breathing. Musculoskeletal: Severe tension in the cervical and thoracic paraspinal muscles. Neurological: Alert and oriented, but speech is pressured. The clinical presentation is dominated by the fraudulence belief; the patient repeatedly attempts to check their work laptop during the physical exam. Over-performance exhaustion is physically evident in the pronounced tremor and dark periorbital circles.
Environmental Observations: Five empty energy drink cans and multiple legal pads are scattered across the living room table.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Reports massive caffeine intake over the last 3 days to sustain work output.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Imposter Phenomenon (Marginalization-Driven) - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Occupational problem) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the reproducible chest wall tenderness (musculoskeletal origin), normal oxygen saturations, and chest tightness clearly secondary to extreme caffeine intake, hyperarousal, and physical exhaustion.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of hypoxemia, clear lung fields, and the specific orthostatic nature of the presyncope driven by dehydration/exhaustion.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the severe anxiety is strictly localized to occupational performance and stereotype threat, resolving when the specific threat is mitigated, rather than globalized worry.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute physiological decompensation, including significant orthostatic hypotension (BP 90/55 on standing) and marked tachycardia (HR 118), driven by severe dehydration, excessive caffeine intake, and massive psychological stress (over-performance exhaustion). Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face evaluation was required to safely exclude an acute cardiac event or dangerous arrhythmia, and to initiate immediate oral rehydration and enforced rest to prevent syncope and secondary head trauma. Delaying care until routine hours would have allowed the physiological crisis to worsen, strictly meeting the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered guided oral rehydration therapy (1 liter of electrolyte solution) on site. Prescribed a 3-day medical certificate for immediate stress leave. Recommended the usual GP assess for elevated scores on the Clance Impostor Phenomenon Scale and salivary cortisol dysregulation indicating chronic stress.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically recover from the over-performance exhaustion, enforce a strict 48-hour "digital quarantine": power down all work devices and hand them to a trusted friend; the physical removal of the devices prevents compulsive checking and forces the brain to initiate rest. To combat the fraudulence belief upon returning to work, implement a "praise journal": physically write down one piece of positive, objective feedback each day; reviewing this factual list weekly counteracts the emotional narrative of being an imposter.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an occupational psychologist specializing in minority stress and stereotype threat.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure reassessment and long-term psychological support planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing crushing central chest pain, actual fainting and loss of consciousness, or a heart rate remaining above 120 bpm after 2 hours of rest.

